# Line endings, declared here once instead of depending on every developer's
# local core.autocrlf (enigma2_player-efi).
#
# Found while fixing enigma2_player-63s: 12 of the 41 files Prettier reported on
# Windows were formatted perfectly and failed on CRLF alone - and permanently,
# because the next checkout restored CRLF. CI checks out LF and never saw any of
# it, so the noise landed on one platform only, where it buried the 29 real
# findings. `endOfLine: "auto"` in .prettierrc made Prettier stop caring; it did
# not give this repository an opinion, so every other tool with one (shfmt,
# clang-format, editors) still met a repository that says nothing.
#
# What this file actually changes: the index was already uniformly LF when it was
# written (287 text files, 16 binary), so `git add --renormalize .` found nothing
# to do. The damage was in the WORKING COPY - with core.autocrlf=true, 138 files
# were checked out as CRLF and four had drifted to mixed endings, among them
# every git hook and every shell script in .aiflow/, .claude/hooks/, .githooks/
# and .beads/hooks/. A hook with CRLF lines is one Git-for-Windows version away
# from `bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M`, and bash is the only interpreter
# those files have.

# The rule: git decides what is text, and text is LF in the object store AND in
# the working copy on every platform - that is what bash, Node and Prettier read
# here, and it is what the extensionless hooks need.
* text=auto eol=lf

# PowerShell is the exception that stays CRLF. These are the Windows half of the
# toolchain (.aiflow/*.ps1, .claude/hooks/*.ps1, docker/run.ps1), run by
# powershell.exe, and CRLF is what Windows PowerShell writes back into them -
# without this line every edit from the PowerShell side would show up as a
# whole-file diff. `text` without `eol=lf` means normalised to LF in the object
# store, CRLF on checkout.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# No .bat or .cmd in the tree today, and the rules are here anyway: a file kind
# that MUST have CRLF cannot be detected after the fact - cmd.exe mis-reads a
# batch file with LF endings, and a label or `goto` that silently falls through
# is not something anybody finds by reading a diff. The binary rules below need
# no such foresight, which is why they only list what exists.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Binary: never converted, never diffed as text. Git's heuristic already detects
# all 16 of these, but a heuristic is not a declaration, and a new image format
# it guesses wrong about would be corrupted on checkout, not merely noisy.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
